		{
			"id":"46ad0b6bb383e724",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"et me summarize this for the user.\nDaniel Defoe's Worst Colonial Sins:\n1. Royal Africa Company agent (1690s) — Directly managed slave trade operations on the Gold Coast. Profit from human trafficking.\n2. Colonial propaganda — Wrote pamphlets defending/ promoting English colonization. UBC thesis titled \"Daniel Defoe as a colonial propagandist\" covers this.\n3. Advocated Transportation Act (1718) — Argued for shipping convicts to colonies as forced labor. His novels Colonel Jack and Moll Flanders romanticize this — criminals \"find salvation\" through colonial transportation.\n4. Pro-slavery rhetoric in novels — His works portray slavery and colonial labor as opportunities for redemption, not exploitation. \"Grateful slaves\" trope.\n5. Profit from forced labor systems — He was personally invested in the colonial economy: trade, transportation, plantation-linked commerce.\nThe irony: The man who wrote \"author protection\" in 1709 was profiting from the most brutal forced labor system in history. His \"property\" argument for books mirrors how slave-owners defined \"property\" in human beings.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-9720,
			"y":-1119,
			"width":660,
			"height":520,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"a2b8cce11cba48cf",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"robinson crusoe is the first book written by a character",
			"styleAttributes":{},
