		{"id":"f57c4cd6b2dbf64e","type":"link","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oeYa_Pcg4hg&t=197s","x":-1115,"y":-2537,"width":640,"height":360},
		{"id":"2585efc3a9b9cf0b","type":"file","file":"projects/ongoing/buttons/Pasted image 20260430061627.png","x":-9820,"y":1232,"width":399,"height":203},
		{"id":"852fe35f4e95a6ec","type":"file","file":"projects/ongoing/buttons/Pasted image 20260430061701.png","x":-10270,"y":1236,"width":400,"height":199},
		{
			"id":"4a1ca1f0377ef017",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Great question — and actually two very different things came up! Here's what I found:\nCritical Analysis of Imagination Library Book Biases\nThe main study is by Jennifer K. Stone, a PhD candidate at University of North Carolina. Her dissertation: \"Reading Power With and Through Dolly Parton's Imagination Library: A Critical Content Analysis\" (2024).\nHer findings (via The College Fix & Meaww):\n- Only 7 of 60 books featured BIPOC characters\n- \"Characters with dis/abilities, non-normative gender identities, or non-normative family structures were erased\"\n- Books promoted \"White Western values\": individualism, work ethic, goal-oriented achievement\n- Specific books criticized:\n  - \"Llama Llama Red Pajama\" — called racist, sexist, homophobic, classist, ableist\n  - Dolly's own \"Coat of Many Colors\" — criticized for promoting \"American Dream\" ideology\n  - One book with a cross on the wall = \"gender normative role\"\n- Positive note: Stone acknowledged the books did include racially diverse characters \"at a rate that could be transformative\"\nDefense: Many internet reactions defended Parton, noting books are free, voluntary, and promote literacy regardless.\nOn \"Rotary Club\" specifically\nI didn't find a \"Rotary Club\" book title on the Imagination Library list. But there IS a Rotary Club connection: Rotary International has partnered with Imagination Library — local Rotary clubs sponsor book mailing costs in their communities.\nWant me to dig deeper into either angle? (˶ᵔ ᵕ ᵔ˶)",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-11000,
			"y":1445,
			"width":460,
			"height":590
		},
		{
			"id":"d23108af64859955",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://imaginationlibrary.com/ca/book-list/",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10770,
			"y":2125,
			"width":430,
			"height":80
		},
		{
			"id":"1371beaea66b6240",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://youtu.be/-J_bVzF2OyE?t=16\nit helped me understand how truly and beautifully interconnected our world really is",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-10230,
			"y":1650,
			"width":360,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"250401e6c722d2d1",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"### 1734)\n\nAlso known as **\"Hogarth’s Act**",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-6585,
			"y":1450,
			"width":520,
			"height":140
		},
		{"id":"7a2224e261f7080c","type":"file","file":"projects/ongoing/buttons/Pasted image 20260430060905.png","x":-1096,"y":-2108,"width":400,"height":211},
		{
			"id":"2948f28df627bb06",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe was first published on  25 April 1719. Its original title was \"The Life and Strange Surprizing Adventures of Robinson Crusoe, of York, Mariner: Who lived Eight and Twenty Years, all alone in an un-inhabited Island on the Coast of America, near the Mouth of the Great River of Oroonoque; Having been cast on Shore by Shipwreck, wherein all the Men perished but himself. With An Account how he was at last as strangely deliver'd by Pyrates. Written by Himself._\"",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1495,
			"y":-3797,
			"width":540,
			"height":240,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ebe1e9430944e499",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"a lot of words for something supposed to grab attention. The clickbait of the time, very much \"true crime\". The books were sold unbound and the binding chosen separately, so I suppose this was the cover. They relied on more words than we do today.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2195,
			"y":-3657,
			"width":680,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"98fa05400360f86f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"- John Bunyan (1678) The Pilgrim's Progress — First major English allegorical novel in first-person, uses \"dream vision\" frame as marketing hook\n- Richard Head (1665-71) The English Rogue — First English picaresque novel in first-person, serialized rogue's memoir based on criminal subculture\n- Daniel Defoe (1719) Robinson Crusoe — Genre-defining fictional autobiography, \"Written by Himself\" marketing trick, based loosely on Alexander Selkirk but never credited\n- Daniel Defoe (1722) Moll Flanders — First-person novel about female criminal life, \"Written from the instance\" framing, researched criminal culture\n- Daniel Defoe (1722) Colonel Jack — Soldier's memoir format, orphan-to-criminal narrative arc, same authenticity marketing formula\n- Daniel Defoe (1724) Roxana — Courtesan's memoir with female agency and moral ambiguity, marketed as \"true account\"\n- Cervantes (1605/1615) Don Quixote — Invented the modern novel, first-person framing + third-person hybrid, presented as translation of Arabic manuscript\n- Samuel Richardson (1740) Pamela — Epistolary novel (letters, not autobiography), \"virtue rewarded\" moral framing, launched domestic fiction genre\n- Anonymous (1700s) The Adventures of a Guinea — Unique object-narrator perspective (a coin travels through society), marketed as \"true account\"\n- Anonymous (1690s-1700s) The Complete London Spy — Early expose journalism, \"true account\" of London's underworld and criminal subculture",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1365,
			"y":-3277,
			"width":990,
			"height":420,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"f12ac0f6ca7ff345",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"defoe would make a habit of this lmao moll flanders, colonel jack,roxana",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":735,
			"y":-3657,
			"width":260,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"18253c3af95b33a7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Based on what I know:\n- Lloyd's of London (1686) — Edward Lloyd opened a coffee house where ship owners, merchants, and underwriters gathered to trade insurance. It became the world's insurance market — essentially a risk marketplace. Revolutionary.\n- The Bank of England (1694) — Created to fund war against France. First central bank in modern sense. Issued paper money, managed national debt, pioneered government-backed currency.\n- The first newspapers & advertising (1660s-1700s) — The Oxford Gazette (1665) became The London Gazette. Classified ads started appearing — \"Lost: a black poodle\" etc. The first newspaper ads were for books, medicines, and services.\n- Joint-stock companies — The East India Company (1600), South Sea Company (1711) — allowed investors to buy shares in ventures. speculation frenzy, later bubble (1720 South Sea Bubble).\n- Coffee houses as information markets — Lloyd's (insurance), Jonathan's (stock trading), Lloyd's was essentially the first information marketplace where risk itself became a tradeable commodity.\n- The first shopping — Fixed-price shops replaced haggling in the 1690s-1700s. The drum and shop became standardized retail spaces. Price tags — revolutionary.\n- Credit systems — The \"book credit\" system where merchants kept accounts with each other. Bills of exchange allowed long-distance trade without moving gold.\n- The advertising breakthrough — Robinson Crusoe's \"Written by Himself\" was part of a broader shift: selling experience rather than product. The same era saw traveling showmen, quack medicines with fake testimonials, and the birth of brand as promise.\nThe pattern: turning information into commodity, risk into market, experience into product. That's what Defoe did with Selkirk's story — and what the whole merchant class was doing with everything else.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2375,
			"y":-2997,
			"width":500,
			"height":400,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"8e61922d45904866",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Its first edition sold a couple thousand copies, it was an immediate success that created a new reading audience among the middle class\n\n- Robinson Crusoe ≈ Gulliver's Travels — both immediate bestsellers, ~1,000 first edition, sold out fast\n- Pamela was bigger — literally created \"first edition fever,\" queues at bookshops, first novel to have modern \"bestseller\" culture\n- The Pilgrim's Progress had longest tail — printed continuously for 200 years, tens of thousands of copies over decades\nThe pattern: Defoe and Swift (1720s) created the commercial novel. Richardson (1740s) made it mass-culture. Pamela was the true breakthrough in terms of \"everyone's talking about it\" cultural moment.\nThe middle class was the market* — they had money (3-6 shillings), leisure to read, and hunger for \"true stories\" (the authenticity marketing Defoe pioneered).",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":735,
			"y":-3357,
			"width":540,
			"height":400,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ced5ceafeac6ea52",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Where did the middle class money come from?\n- Merchants & traders — Domestic and international trade, East India Company, colonial trade\n- Shopkeepers & retailers — Fixed-price shops replacing markets, selling manufactured goods\n- Professionals — Lawyers, doctors, clergymen, architects, surveyors\n- Bankers & financiers — The City of London, early credit systems\n- Artisans & master craftsmen — Skilled workers, especially in growing manufacturing towns\n- Sea captains & ship owners — Maritime commerce\n- Government officials — Growing bureaucracy\n- Industrialists — Early mining, manufacturing (textiles in Lancashire, iron in Midlands)\nWhere did they live?\n- London (biggest market for books)\n- Port cities: Bristol, Liverpool, Newcastle, Hull\n- Growing market towns\n- Manufacturing centers: Birmingham, Manchester (early)\nWhat was their money worth?\n- Laborer: ~1-2 shillings/week\n- Skilled craftsman: ~2-5 shillings/week\n- Merchant/professional: ~10-50+ shillings/week\nA 3-6 shilling book was affordable for the top 10-20% of society — not the masses, but the emerging commercial/professional class that had:\n- Disposable income (after food, housing)\n- Leisure time (not working from dawn to dusk)\n- Aspiration (self-improvement, \"polite\" culture)\n- Social anxiety (wanting to distinguish from \"lower\" classes)\nThey wanted respectable entertainment — novels presented as \"true accounts\" fit perfectly. It was improving as well as entertaining.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":525,
			"y":-2857,
			"width":480,
			"height":340,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"339230b270ddd7cc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The prosperity came from:\n1. Colonial/Atlantic Trade\n- East India Company — spices, textiles, tea, porcelain\n- Atlantic triangle — sugar, tobacco, cotton, rum, slaves\n- The slave trade itself — major profits feeding back to London, Bristol, Liverpool\n2. Domestic Manufacturing\n- Textiles — wool from Yorkshire, cotton emerging in Lancashire\n- Iron — Birmingham, Sheffield (weapons, tools)\n- Coal — Newcastle, powering everything\n3. Enclosure of Common Land\n- Landed g enclosing common fields → evicting peasants\n- Those displaced became workers, or migrated\n- Enclosure made landowners richer, created labor pool\n4. Financial Revolution\n- Bank of England (1694) — credit, national debt, paper money\n- Joint-stock companies — speculation, bubbles\n- Lloyd's — insurance for ships\n5. War Economy\n- Government contracts for army/navy supplies\n- Debt created financial instruments\n- Some made fortunes from supplying wars\n---\nThe dirty secret:\nThe middle class prosperity that let them buy Robinson Crusoe was directly connected to:\n- Slave trade profits (London merchants profited from the triangular trade)\n- Colonial extraction (sugar, tobacco plantations)\n- Enclosure dispossession (peasants pushed off land → labor pool)\n- War-profiteering (contracts, speculation)\nSo the very people reading Defoe's \"author protection\" rhetoric were often profiting from the actual extraction — of labor, land, and colonial subjects.\nThat connection is exactly what you're building into the story, right?",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":525,
			"y":-2377,
			"width":700,
			"height":240,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"8bec1a05410eb265",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"the war between words and images ??",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":2875,
			"y":-3307,
			"width":260,
			"height":60,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"faf3620850641360",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Robinson Crusoe is credited for the rise of realism. It was one of the first times writing shifted away from fantastical legends and epic poems. Contrary to what came before, it shifted the focus on the individual.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1435,
			"y":-4057,
			"width":660,
			"height":180,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"ee92cfbfbbb3a312",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"let me tell you about a human. \na white male.\nOne of those names you probably know so well from having heard it a thousand times, so you don't even notice or pause to wonder anymore.\nWho was Daniel Defoe, the author of Robinson Crusoe, by some measures the first english novel.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":1535,
			"y":-4317,
			"width":500,
			"height":160,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"5f23c6534927a79d",
