		{
			"id":"bad180016d3832e4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H_aOHpn_vqQ\njohn locke\nanne statute\nHogarth act 1734\n\n1. The Stationers' Company — after failing for 15 years to restore their monopoly, they pivoted to arguing \"for authors\" in 1709\n\nDaniel Defoe — published the demand in December 1709 in his \"A Review,\" but he was working for the Tory government at the time\n\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worshipful_Company_of_Stationers_and_Newspaper_Makers\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7720,
			"y":307,
			"width":1020,
			"height":340
		},
		{
			"id":"cc521d00f1380c50",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"whats this list edward darrel, charles harper, robert andrews, freeman collins, samuel roycroft, john baskett, thomas hodgkin, william phillips\n\nThis list consists of notable **English printers and booksellers**, specifically those active during the late 17th and early 18th centuries.\n\nWhile they may not all be household names today, many of them held the prestigious title of **King’s Printer** or were key figures in the **Stationers' Company** in London. This group was responsible for producing official government documents, law books, and the earliest \"mass-market\" literature in England.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-7460,
			"y":-120,
			"width":460,
			"height":240
		},
		{
			"id":"26fd431168a03ad5",
